From 8301de94a4215d0e728f3a103f092349bf702782 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alessandro Fontana <alessandro.fontana@pm.me>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 18:07:12 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/exynos: decon7: clear the windows the block resumes with

The SysMMU raises an unrecoverable read fault for the display controller
a few seconds after the panel wakes up, and it survives 0101: the window
disable at suspend is now latched, and the fault still happens at the
same rate (one in 20 cycles before, one in 14 after).

The suspend path is therefore not where the stale configuration comes
from. decon_init() resets the block and programs VIDOUTCON0, VCLKCON0,
VIDCON1, BLENDCON and WINCHMAP0, but it clears no window: whatever
WINCONx_ENWIN and VIDWxxADD0 the block had before the suspend are still
there when decon_atomic_flush() releases the protection of every window
and starts the scanout. The window is latched as it was, DECON fetches
the buffer it had before the suspend, and by then userspace may well
have released it - which is the fault:

  exynos-sysmmu 14860000.iommu: 14830000.display-controller: [READ]
  PAGE FAULT occurred at 0xfde01fc0
  Kernel panic - not syncing: Unrecoverable System MMU Fault!

decon_clear_channels() already does exactly this clearing, but it runs
once at probe, against what the bootloader left behind; a resume needs
the same treatment. Do it after the reset, while the scanout is still
off, so no vsync wait is needed.
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/exynos/exynos7_drm_decon.c | 21 +++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/exynos/exynos7_drm_decon.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/exynos/exynos7_drm_decon.c
index 7e8bfe4cd3..01064c6473 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/exynos/exynos7_drm_decon.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/exynos/exynos7_drm_decon.c
@@ -720,6 +720,27 @@ static void decon_init(struct decon_context *ctx)
 		val |= WINCHMAP_DMA(win, win);
 	}
 	writel(val, ctx->regs + WINCHMAP0);
+
+	/*
+	 * Neither the reset nor the programming above clears the per-window
+	 * configuration. decon_clear_channels() does that, but it only runs
+	 * at probe, against what the bootloader left behind. Do the same here
+	 * so that a window left enabled before the suspend cannot be latched
+	 * again by the unprotect in decon_atomic_flush(): it would point at a
+	 * buffer that userspace has since released, and DECON would fetch it
+	 * as soon as scanout resumes.
+	 */
+	for (win = 0; win < WINDOWS_NR; win++) {
+		decon_shadow_protect_win(ctx, win, true);
+		val = readl(ctx->regs + WINCON(win));
+		val &= ~WINCONx_ENWIN;
+		writel(val, ctx->regs + WINCON(win));
+		decon_shadow_protect_win(ctx, win, false);
+	}
+
+	val = readl(ctx->regs + DECON_UPDATE);
+	val |= DECON_UPDATE_STANDALONE_F;
+	writel(val, ctx->regs + DECON_UPDATE);
 }
 
 static void decon_atomic_enable(struct exynos_drm_crtc *crtc)
-- 
